{"timestamp": "2026-09-13T20:35:04.382450+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-383fbf91-6007-4fbf-8642-4aaa2bdf090c', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You author one already-selected educational capability. Follow the\\nsupplied AuthoringDefinition and exact output schema. Preserve block identity, objective, scope,\\nlearner action, source ownership and assessment mode. Do not select another capability or redesign\\nthe teaching plan.\\n\\nPlanning instructions are not final content. For an activity, produce a meaningful prompt, task\\ndata, justified answer relationships and useful feedback as required by the contract. Do not infer\\ncorrectness from option positions, number/word positions, example ordering or arbitrary defaults.\\nDo not copy illustrative examples as unrelated lesson content.\\n\\nTreat approved items as authoritative content. Conversion must preserve their question and answer\\nmeaning. Report incompatibility through the defined error path instead of guessing. Use supported\\nteacher-review only when the work order policy permits it.\\n\\nReturn exactly the selected output schema. Do not emit sibling schemas, markdown wrappers, internal\\nplanning metadata or extra fields. If required information is absent, use the configured\\nmissing-input mechanism; do not create placeholder content to satisfy structure.\\n\\nDo not invent or overwrite knowledge/assessment policy fields. Effective policy is code-owned.\\n\\n## KNOWLEDGE POLICY\\nKnowledge policy: use supplied facts as the primary basis when present; do not describe model knowledge as approved factual material.\\n\\n## PACKAGE CAPABILITY INSTRUCTIONS\\nYou write LEARNER-FACING content for ONE ordinary document primitive.\\n\\nAllowed kinds: paragraph, heading, list, table, callout, figure.\\n(Figure: write caption + alt only. Asset generation uses the figure pipeline.)\\n\\nHard rules:\\n- Fill ONLY fields for the assigned kind. No unrelated fields.\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\"Row 1\"), generic headers (\"Item\"/\"Detail\"),\\n  or \"Content pending.\"\\n\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\n- paragraph \u2192 { \"text\": string }\\n- heading \u2192 { \"text\": string, \"level\": 1|2|3 }\\n- list \u2192 { \"ordered\": boolean, \"items\": [string, ...] }\\n- table \u2192 { \"headers\": [string, ...], \"rows\": [[string, ...], ...], \"caption\": string }\\n- callout \u2192 { \"tone\": \"note\"|\"warning\"|\"tip\"|\"important\", \"title\": string, \"body\": string }\\n- figure \u2192 { \"caption\": string, \"alt\": string }\\n\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\n\\n\\n## AUTHORING DEFINITION\\n{\\n  \"assessment\": {},\\n  \"capability_id\": \"document-writer:paragraph\",\\n  \"converter_ref\": null,\\n  \"definition_hash\": \"c24bc83c1b6259ec1b3c6c18b2e06a94499913121902ea0dcab3b68c3ffafde1:paragraph\",\\n  \"instructions\": \"You write LEARNER-FACING content for ONE ordinary document primitive.\\\\n\\\\nAllowed kinds: paragraph, heading, list, table, callout, figure.\\\\n(Figure: write caption + alt only. Asset generation uses the figure pipeline.)\\\\n\\\\nHard rules:\\\\n- Fill ONLY fields for the assigned kind. No unrelated fields.\\\\n- No component_id, template_id, page_break, ruled_lines, or media objects.\\\\n- Do not author interactions; those use the interaction writer.\\\\n- The Teaching Plan brief is a WRITER INSTRUCTION, not student-facing text.\\\\n  Never copy the brief verbatim into the learner-facing fields.\\\\n- Honour objective, terminology, evidence / approved facts, and must_not_introduce.\\\\n- Do not reteach prior_established content; reference it briefly when useful.\\\\n- Stay coherent with neighbouring composition choices when provided.\\\\n- Do not invent placeholder rows (\\\\\"Row 1\\\\\"), generic headers (\\\\\"Item\\\\\"/\\\\\"Detail\\\\\"),\\\\n  or \\\\\"Content pending.\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nPrimitive field contracts:\\\\n- paragraph \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string }\\\\n- heading \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"text\\\\\": string, \\\\\"level\\\\\": 1|2|3 }\\\\n- list \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"ordered\\\\\": boolean, \\\\\"items\\\\\": [string, ...] }\\\\n- table \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"headers\\\\\": [string, ...], \\\\\"rows\\\\\": [[string, ...], ...], \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string }\\\\n- callout \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"tone\\\\\": \\\\\"note\\\\\"|\\\\\"warning\\\\\"|\\\\\"tip\\\\\"|\\\\\"important\\\\\", \\\\\"title\\\\\": string, \\\\\"body\\\\\": string }\\\\n- figure \\\\u2192 { \\\\\"caption\\\\\": string, \\\\\"alt\\\\\": string }\\\\n\\\\nReturn JSON matching the assigned primitive schema only (plus kind).\\\\n\",\\n  \"knowledge\": {},\\n  \"modes\": [\\n    \"generate\"\\n  ],\\n  \"native_path\": \"document\",\\n  \"payload_schema\": {\\n    \"additionalProperties\": false,\\n    \"properties\": {\\n      \"kind\": {\\n        \"const\": \"paragraph\"\\n      },\\n      \"text\": {\\n        \"minLength\": 1,\\n        \"type\": \"string\"\\n      }\\n    },\\n    \"required\": [\\n      \"kind\",\\n      \"text\"\\n    ],\\n    \"type\": \"object\"\\n  },\\n  \"required_inputs\": [\\n    \"kind\",\\n    \"brief\",\\n    \"teaching_block\"\\n  ],\\n  \"validator_refs\": [\\n    \"document.writer_schema\"\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n## SCOPED REQUEST\\n{\\n  \"inputs\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Set the water-as-food reading against the target on the same windowsill plant beside the cup of water: one learner says the plant\\'s food is the water we give it, since water is what we pour in; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and the water is an intake, not the food. Make clear that pouring more water does not feed the plant in the sense of food, and that the plant remains the maker. Do not re-run the soil argument and do not treat sunlight as food or as energy here.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availability\": \"supplied_statements\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food\",\\n      \"subject\": \"Science\",\\n      \"teaching_plan_revision\": 1,\\n      \"title\": \"The lesson opens on the classroom plant on the windowsill beside the cup of water and asks learners to commit to a guess about where its food comes from, so a personal wrong model is on the table. The explanation then fixes the category term \\\\u2014 the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food \\\\u2014 and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.\"\\n    },\\n    \"neighbours\": [\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Puts the soil-as-food reading beside the producer idea and shows why the soil reading cannot stand.\",\\n        \"role\": \"contrast\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"callout\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Surfaces whether the learner still holds the soil-as-food model by prompting a choice between the two readings.\",\\n        \"role\": \"diagnostic-prompt\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"list\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Lays out the water-as-food claim against the producer idea so the boundary of the concept is visible.\",\\n        \"role\": \"comparison\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b2\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n        \"reason\": \"Poses one application check about where the windowsill plant\\'s food comes from, keeping to a single recognition of the plant as producer.\",\\n        \"role\": \"check-prompt\",\\n        \"teaching_block_id\": \"check-b1\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"reason\": \"Sets the water-as-food reading against the producer idea on the same plant beside the cup of water.\",\\n    \"role\": \"contrast\",\\n    \"teaching_block\": {\\n      \"brief\": \"Set the water-as-food reading against the target on the same windowsill plant beside the cup of water: one learner says the plant\\'s food is the water we give it, since water is what we pour in; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and the water is an intake, not the food. Make clear that pouring more water does not feed the plant in the sense of food, and that the plant remains the maker. Do not re-run the soil argument and do not treat sunlight as food or as energy here.\",\\n      \"evidence\": \"M2 is a second named belief that places food outside the plant, and because the anchor already includes the cup of water, this block can test that belief against a case the learner has held since the opening instead of a fresh example.\",\\n      \"id\": \"contrast-b2\",\\n      \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\"\\n    },\\n    \"terminology\": []\\n  },\\n  \"mode\": \"generate\",\\n  \"scoped_request\": {\\n    \"allowed_facts\": [\\n      \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\",\\n      \"living things need food to grow\",\\n      \"Plants are living things that can produce their own food\",\\n      \"Plants are called producers\",\\n      \"Use one concrete classroom example.\",\\n      \"Explain that plants make their own food (are producers)\",\\n      \"Identify leaves as the site of photosynthesis and chlorophyll as the light-capturing substance\",\\n      \"Describe photosynthesis as using light, carbon dioxide, and water to produce food and oxygen\",\\n      \"Explain why light is essential for photosynthesis\"\\n    ],\\n    \"brief\": \"Set the water-as-food reading against the target on the same windowsill plant beside the cup of water: one learner says the plant\\'s food is the water we give it, since water is what we pour in; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and the water is an intake, not the food. Make clear that pouring more water does not feed the plant in the sense of food, and that the plant remains the maker. Do not re-run the soil argument and do not treat sunlight as food or as energy here.\",\\n    \"evidence\": \"M2 is a second named belief that places food outside the plant, and because the anchor already includes the cup of water, this block can test that belief against a case the learner has held since the opening instead of a fresh example.\",\\n    \"kind\": \"paragraph\",\\n    \"lesson_context\": {\\n      \"input_availabi